1
00:00:04,247 --> 00:00:11,809
[SPEAKER_01]: What about mindfulness?

2
00:00:11,829 --> 00:00:25,031
[SPEAKER_02]: So, composure is a result of mindfulness?

3
00:00:25,432 --> 00:00:32,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, composure is a result, can be a result, or rather, the right composure is the result of dispassion.

4
00:00:34,016 --> 00:00:39,739
[SPEAKER_02]: Disenchantment, nibbida, disgust towards the world, towards the senses, sensual pleasures.

5
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:41,801
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the composure you want.

6
00:00:42,801 --> 00:00:49,625
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want some sort of contemporary concentration technique or something like that.

7
00:00:49,725 --> 00:00:55,649
[SPEAKER_02]: You want composure based on dispassion, based on turning away from the desires towards the world.

8
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:01,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness of death provides you with that, but as

9
00:01:02,102 --> 00:01:08,567
[SPEAKER_02]: As it was the case in the suttas, it's a bit... well, you can push some people over the edge.

10
00:01:08,867 --> 00:01:11,128
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, the Buddha offered the alternative, i.e.

11
00:01:11,148 --> 00:01:20,795
[SPEAKER_02]: the same thing, just in the form of mindfulness of breathing, just in the form of a less personal approach.

12
00:01:21,135 --> 00:01:22,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, in mindfulness of that, you begin,

13
00:01:28,039 --> 00:01:29,400
[SPEAKER_02]: Thinking through your point of view.

14
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:34,303
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness of breathing, you take the breathing as a point of view, not yourself.

15
00:01:35,243 --> 00:01:37,485
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you are breathing in like this, I'm breathing like that.

16
00:01:37,885 --> 00:01:43,989
[SPEAKER_02]: But basically, the fact that the Buddha offered it instead of maraṇasati means it's that thing.

17
00:01:44,089 --> 00:01:45,329
[SPEAKER_02]: It pertains to that thing.

18
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:51,013
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you do mindfulness of breathing rightly, you are basically contemplating death rightly.

19
00:01:51,693 --> 00:01:54,635
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're experiencing dispassion towards the world rightly.

20
00:01:57,528 --> 00:02:01,792
[SPEAKER_01]: He said to Āceta Sūta, he was teaching the Subha meditation.

21
00:02:01,872 --> 00:02:05,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, it was Subha, wasn't it?

22
00:02:05,635 --> 00:02:05,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

23
00:02:05,975 --> 00:02:06,616
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

24
00:02:06,796 --> 00:02:08,217
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

25
00:02:08,997 --> 00:02:10,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

26
00:02:10,098 --> 00:02:14,422
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I was trying to basically... Subha and death, kind of, just discussed.

27
00:02:14,662 --> 00:02:18,565
[SPEAKER_02]: But, as I said, it comes to me and this body and I am.

28
00:02:19,266 --> 00:02:23,569
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if done rightly, the Buddha said, it's like, bear fruit in no time.

29
00:02:23,589 --> 00:02:25,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Full dispassion right away.

30
00:02:25,491 --> 00:02:25,751
[SPEAKER_02]: But,

31
00:02:26,414 --> 00:02:38,205
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people might get a bit too much, and then they'll think, like, oh, I'm better off without this body, and then they'll be jumping off the cliffs and... and asking those other... that crazy man to kill them with a sword, and... and so on.

32
00:02:40,507 --> 00:02:42,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I was going to say this.

33
00:02:42,189 --> 00:02:45,552
[SPEAKER_01]: What's... what's the end goal of the marathon, the training?

34
00:02:50,931 --> 00:02:56,374
[SPEAKER_02]: All of that is basically rooted or springs from correct dispassion.

35
00:02:57,395 --> 00:03:00,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you feel no desire towards the world, towards life.

36
00:03:02,698 --> 00:03:05,440
[SPEAKER_02]: And also, you don't feel any desire towards death either.

37
00:03:07,942 --> 00:03:08,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is it?

38
00:03:09,403 --> 00:03:11,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because death is the sort of

39
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:16,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the notion of death requires, as a basis, the notion of life that you have.

40
00:03:17,610 --> 00:03:21,752
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's when people... when people are involved with the life, that's when they want death.

41
00:03:22,933 --> 00:03:32,198
[SPEAKER_02]: When that life sort of doesn't go their way, or goes... or it's actually unpleasant, then they want death.

42
00:03:32,278 --> 00:03:35,500
[SPEAKER_02]: With the same desire, they were wanting life, now they want the ending of life.

43
00:03:37,168 --> 00:03:42,509
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you feel dispassion towards that life, you feel dispassion towards... you don't care whether that life is there or not.

44
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:44,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, you don't care whether you're alive or not.

45
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,370
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, you cannot possibly be desiring death either.

46
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:49,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why would you be dispassionate towards life?

47
00:03:50,211 --> 00:03:50,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

48
00:03:50,691 --> 00:03:53,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you be ex-vijānta?

49
00:03:53,351 --> 00:03:54,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's... What?

50
00:03:57,892 --> 00:04:02,333
[SPEAKER_01]: What way do you see, like, you watch the breath and you become dispassionate towards life, you know?

51
00:04:03,395 --> 00:04:04,396
[SPEAKER_02]: You see that the body's...

52
00:04:04,557 --> 00:04:07,340
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you mean, how will breath make you dispassionate towards life?

53
00:04:08,141 --> 00:04:08,381
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

54
00:04:08,722 --> 00:04:15,891
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, understanding that your life can end at any next in-breath or out-breath without your control.

55
00:04:17,192 --> 00:04:19,916
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you actually have to take that idea

56
00:04:21,485 --> 00:04:23,067
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to think about the breath.

57
00:04:23,127 --> 00:04:24,188
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just be watching.

58
00:04:24,288 --> 00:04:25,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, of course.

59
00:04:25,289 --> 00:04:27,532
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to be thinking about what is going on here.

60
00:04:27,592 --> 00:04:28,853
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

61
00:04:29,093 --> 00:04:36,221
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, I said millions of times before, I'm not talking about modern vipassana techniques, or Burmese, or whatnot, or even what people think mindfulness of breathing is.

62
00:04:36,321 --> 00:04:36,662
[SPEAKER_02]: I think.

63
00:04:37,162 --> 00:04:37,783
[SPEAKER_02]: No, not at all.

64
00:04:37,923 --> 00:04:38,664
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all nonsense.

65
00:04:38,824 --> 00:04:41,507
[SPEAKER_02]: You read the suttas, and the Buddha's quite clear how you do the breathing.

66
00:04:42,328 --> 00:04:43,829
[SPEAKER_02]: You breathe in like this, you breathe in like that.

67
00:04:44,009 --> 00:04:49,774
[SPEAKER_02]: So basically, you're actively involving your volition in the way how you breathe while you're being mindful of that breath.

68
00:04:49,814 --> 00:04:50,054
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

69
00:04:50,615 --> 00:05:02,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you do that steadily enough, it will reveal you that no amount of volition can cross that threshold after which the breath becomes in your control.

70
00:05:02,945 --> 00:05:06,888
[SPEAKER_02]: Or rather, the life that is provided by the breath

71
00:05:09,381 --> 00:05:10,642
[SPEAKER_02]: That's impossible.

72
00:05:11,203 --> 00:05:20,993
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only control the manner of your breathing, but the way that your life depends upon your breath, that's completely inconceivable for you to control, to access, to have any input on.

73
00:05:21,013 --> 00:05:22,014
[SPEAKER_02]: You could just be breathing.

74
00:05:28,107 --> 00:05:51,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Always afraid that no amount of control that you're right now exerting here, breathing in slowly, breathing in relaxingly, breathing in like this, and breathing in like that, and breathing in pleasantly, no amount of that control can sort of obscure or prevent the clarity of the life can end the very next in-breath or out-breath.

75
00:05:53,305 --> 00:05:55,407
[SPEAKER_02]: You just cannot cross into that

76
00:05:56,417 --> 00:06:08,340
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's how you also uproot the notion of self, the notion of me and mine in regard to your own volition, which is the hardest thing to do, because that's the closest to you.

77
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:12,781
[SPEAKER_02]: That intention, that direction, the point of view.

78
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:21,803
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you see no amount... and you do that long enough, like mindfully, willingly, intentionally, yeah, you're leaving nothing out.

79
00:06:22,526 --> 00:06:26,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever is there, you're seeing as dependent upon that which is not in your control.

80
00:06:27,327 --> 00:06:30,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is basically that breath.

81
00:06:31,388 --> 00:06:34,708
[SPEAKER_02]: The life aspect in that breath, that's not in your control.

82
00:06:34,968 --> 00:06:36,628
[SPEAKER_02]: The manner of your breathing, you can control.

83
00:06:37,029 --> 00:06:39,749
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact that your life depends upon that breath, you cannot control.

84
00:06:41,209 --> 00:06:42,369
[SPEAKER_02]: That bodily breath?

85
00:06:42,910 --> 00:06:44,710
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that very bodily breath.

86
00:06:44,770 --> 00:06:47,070
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the organs and air moving and everything else.

87
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:51,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is... See, if I want my attention to breathe,

88
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:54,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I actually watch my intention to breathe, you say?

89
00:06:54,981 --> 00:06:58,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you watch that by breathing, by being mindful of your breath.

90
00:06:58,943 --> 00:07:00,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, there's a tension to breathe.

91
00:07:00,784 --> 00:07:01,044
Yeah.

92
00:07:01,284 --> 00:07:02,085
In a particular way.

93
00:07:02,285 --> 00:07:05,126
I can say I'm breathing in, calming the body.

94
00:07:05,766 --> 00:07:12,489
[SPEAKER_01]: The result of that, what I see, coming at the same time, is my... is the body breathing.

95
00:07:12,509 --> 00:07:15,771
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, tension is present there simultaneously with the body breathing in such manner.

96
00:07:15,851 --> 00:07:16,051
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

97
00:07:16,231 --> 00:07:18,552
[SPEAKER_01]: But how does the intention look?

98
00:07:19,292 --> 00:07:20,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Like an intention.

99
00:07:20,753 --> 00:07:21,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

100
00:07:23,902 --> 00:07:30,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I get confused whether am I now paying attention to the actual body breathing?

101
00:07:30,424 --> 00:07:31,024
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

102
00:07:32,384 --> 00:07:33,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Why am I paying attention?

103
00:07:33,244 --> 00:07:37,985
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter why you're paying attention, as long as you're including both things.

104
00:07:39,246 --> 00:07:46,487
[SPEAKER_02]: Your attention can be paying more attention to the bodily aspect or the I am breathing aspect.

105
00:07:46,507 --> 00:07:47,008
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter.

106
00:07:48,068 --> 00:07:48,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Where is it going?

107
00:07:49,145 --> 00:07:49,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter.

108
00:07:49,805 --> 00:07:59,028
[SPEAKER_02]: As long as both things are included and nothing is left out in regard to seeing that fundamental dependence upon that which is not in your control.

109
00:08:00,689 --> 00:08:07,611
[SPEAKER_02]: Organs, oxygen present in the breath, whatever you want to call it, whatever you want to label it, whatever you want to designate it, doesn't matter.

110
00:08:07,731 --> 00:08:15,793
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is that your intentional breathing and the fact that your life depends upon it, you can control only so far.

111
00:08:17,014 --> 00:08:17,834
[SPEAKER_02]: Ultimately, you can't.

112
00:08:20,935 --> 00:08:28,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, basically, you're just keeping it, keeping it active.

113
00:08:48,801 --> 00:08:52,763
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to give it a directive to remain mindful of your very volition.

114
00:08:53,503 --> 00:08:56,204
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the moment you slip into a method or a technique, you're done.

115
00:08:56,744 --> 00:08:57,885
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness cannot be there.

116
00:08:59,746 --> 00:09:02,447
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't mean that every next breath you have to do differently.

117
00:09:03,067 --> 00:09:07,709
[SPEAKER_02]: All those are just listed, the ways you can be doing it while remaining mindful.

118
00:09:07,749 --> 00:09:12,331
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, within that mindfulness of the general, kind of,

119
00:09:15,417 --> 00:09:22,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Of the fact, of the general fact, that you're being undermined, basically, by the nature of your life's dependence upon breath.

120
00:09:23,022 --> 00:09:24,043
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot access that.

121
00:09:24,863 --> 00:09:38,752
[SPEAKER_02]: You can be breathing as softly, smoothly, and as controlling as you can be, but the dependence, the phenomenon that your life depends upon it, upon the presence of that breath, that's something you cannot control.

122
00:09:38,792 --> 00:09:39,612
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just there.

123
00:09:40,333 --> 00:09:43,455
[SPEAKER_02]: And if that changes, no amount of your breathing will keep you alive.

124
00:09:49,328 --> 00:09:53,270
[SPEAKER_02]: Think of it like, you keep breathing normally, but suddenly there's no oxygen in your breathing.

125
00:09:53,290 --> 00:09:56,291
[SPEAKER_02]: You're breathing some, I don't know, carbon monoxide.

126
00:09:56,932 --> 00:10:05,735
[SPEAKER_02]: You're still breathing, but you're not actually going to be alive for much longer, despite your same volition, same efforts and everything.

127
00:10:05,775 --> 00:10:11,218
[SPEAKER_02]: But these external circumstances, external form that your life depends upon, has changed.

128
00:10:11,318 --> 00:10:13,519
[SPEAKER_02]: And you cannot, you cannot have an input in that.

129
00:10:13,939 --> 00:10:16,440
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot breathe in in such a manner to, like,

130
00:10:18,127 --> 00:10:23,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Maintain your life, so to speak, despite the absence of oxygen in the air you're breathing.

131
00:10:27,174 --> 00:10:30,916
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, the aspects upon which your life depends are out of your control.

132
00:10:31,677 --> 00:10:43,045
[SPEAKER_02]: But, not knowing that clearly enough, the notion of control that you have, or rather, the notion of volition that we have, that we can exercise, induce the notion of ultimate control.

133
00:10:43,743 --> 00:10:47,284
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you think you're breathing because you're alive, because your life is in your control.

134
00:10:47,744 --> 00:10:54,745
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if you suddenly end up somewhere where there is no oxygen to breathe in, you thought that was an unfortunate incident or something.

135
00:10:54,765 --> 00:11:00,926
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact is that such things are possible means that your life is rooted outside of your control.

136
00:11:00,986 --> 00:11:05,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you are truly in your control, no incidents or accidents could happen to you of any kind.

137
00:11:07,167 --> 00:11:10,528
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, breath, obviously, is the most personal action you're doing.

138
00:11:10,648 --> 00:11:11,628
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's a good

139
00:11:13,035 --> 00:11:14,175
[SPEAKER_02]: way to reveal this.

140
00:11:14,636 --> 00:11:24,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, really, your whole life, like, you can be breathing and breathing, but... and, like, okay, forget about it, and also, let's say, you don't... you don't know any chemistry and stuff, so you don't think about oxygen or carbon monoxide or whatever else.

141
00:11:25,219 --> 00:11:31,581
[SPEAKER_02]: Just have the phenomenon, like... like mindfulness of the clear air passage in your body.

142
00:11:32,341 --> 00:11:33,501
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what your life depends on.

143
00:11:34,102 --> 00:11:37,443
[SPEAKER_02]: And if something obstructs it internally, your life's ending.

144
00:11:37,963 --> 00:11:38,243
[SPEAKER_02]: Done.

145
00:11:38,703 --> 00:11:39,843
[SPEAKER_02]: And you have no say in it.

146
00:11:40,464 --> 00:11:41,644
[SPEAKER_02]: If your heart stops pumping,

147
00:11:42,198 --> 00:11:43,819
[SPEAKER_02]: No amount of breathing can help you.

148
00:11:44,740 --> 00:11:48,682
[SPEAKER_02]: If your lungs get constricted, or collapse, or something, you're done.

149
00:11:49,143 --> 00:11:53,505
[SPEAKER_02]: If something just blocks, blocks these tubes, you're done.

150
00:11:54,986 --> 00:12:00,310
[SPEAKER_02]: And your great volition of this, like that, it's all secondary to it, and that's what you want to see.

151
00:12:02,011 --> 00:12:02,511
[SPEAKER_02]: It extends.

152
00:12:02,671 --> 00:12:07,895
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, you do mindfulness rightly, full dispassion, full recognition of death, the nature of death.

153
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:12,302
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, urgency is automatic.

154
00:12:14,925 --> 00:12:17,007
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the full extent of your control.

155
00:12:17,767 --> 00:12:18,008
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

156
00:12:19,809 --> 00:12:24,353
[SPEAKER_02]: And still being secondary to the fact that you depend upon that which is not in your control.

157
00:12:26,555 --> 00:12:29,978
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you see your control as not being in your control.

158
00:12:34,622 --> 00:12:35,423
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I do, yeah.

159
00:12:36,763 --> 00:12:37,744
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the one.

160
00:12:37,944 --> 00:12:42,147
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why the Buddha gave that as an alternative to a suit by maraṇasati and so on.

161
00:12:46,250 --> 00:12:46,871
[SPEAKER_01]: My, eh...

162
00:12:47,791 --> 00:12:51,694
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose the understanding of this also is like, you know, a city, a marana in a city.

163
00:12:52,875 --> 00:13:01,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, if you just try and do it off the bat, you still have all the other assumptions and, you know, all the things, like, for example, of, like, assuming material in my thoughts or whatever kind of thing.

164
00:13:02,342 --> 00:13:04,784
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, that's why, I think, you have to come first.

165
00:13:05,773 --> 00:13:09,354
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, well, no, that's what we assumed in this talk, now.

166
00:13:10,054 --> 00:13:11,274
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

167
00:13:11,314 --> 00:13:13,274
[SPEAKER_02]: You can do anything wrongly, anyway.

168
00:13:13,375 --> 00:13:16,095
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, yeah, you need to have the right view.

169
00:13:16,135 --> 00:13:20,616
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if you do it like this, it will lead to the result of full dispersion.

170
00:13:21,496 --> 00:13:22,536
[SPEAKER_02]: It won't lead you in itself.

171
00:13:23,476 --> 00:13:29,138
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, if you do maraṇasati correctly, yeah, it will lead you to arahant.

172
00:13:29,858 --> 00:13:31,598
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't do it correctly, it won't.

173
00:13:35,376 --> 00:13:38,959
[SPEAKER_00]: In samadhanasati, sotā, basically, it's also the same principle.

174
00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:43,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, you get more and more seeing the general level of your experience.

175
00:13:45,485 --> 00:13:45,965
[SPEAKER_00]: In what way?

176
00:13:45,985 --> 00:13:49,228
[SPEAKER_00]: When you... First, you just see, like, breathing in and breathing out.

177
00:13:49,268 --> 00:13:50,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, see the body.

178
00:13:50,048 --> 00:13:50,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, see the formation.

179
00:13:50,869 --> 00:13:52,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, see all the feelings.

180
00:13:52,370 --> 00:13:52,971
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the mind.

181
00:13:53,011 --> 00:13:53,271
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

182
00:13:53,311 --> 00:13:53,491
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

183
00:13:53,551 --> 00:13:55,633
[SPEAKER_02]: You, you, as I said before, you can't do it.

184
00:13:55,713 --> 00:13:56,774
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't mean you have to.

185
00:13:56,894 --> 00:13:58,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Every next breath, do it differently.

186
00:13:58,916 --> 00:14:01,178
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just be breathing, as long as you're breathing mindfully.

187
00:14:01,358 --> 00:14:02,639
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a matter of choice, so far.

